o two-digit subtracting or one-digit subtracting?\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Or three-digit?\nTurn 23. TUTOR: If you can't remember, that's okay.\nTurn 24. STUDENT: Mmm.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: You can't remember?\nTurn 26. TUTOR: No, that's okay. Don't worry about it.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: Okay, we're gonna do a bunch of random things today just to catch up, okay?\nTurn 28. TUTOR: So I have these warm-up problems. We're gonna spend five minutes and I want you to, um, answer them. But I'm gonna go one by one to tell you how I want you to answer them, okay?\nTurn 29. TUTOR: So for number one, I want you to circle the answer. For number two, I want you to write the answer right here. For number three, you can write the answer right here.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: For number four, I want you to circle or highlight. When I say highlight, I mean like this. Like you can highlight it like this. Or, and then for number five, I want you to write the answer right here.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: That sound good?\nTurn 32. TUTOR: Okay, we're gonna spend five minutes starting now.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 14 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student circles the hexagon in problem 1.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 38 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student draws a stick figure and a box under problem 2.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 36 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - writing] Student writes \"42\" next to their drawing for problem 2.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - writing] Student writes \"1/3\" as the answer to problem 3.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 48 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - circling] Student circles the last heart in problem 4.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 129 seconds]\nTurn 33. TUTOR: Okay, you have about two more minutes to do the last problem.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Try your best, even if you think it's wrong.\nTurn 35. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 10 seconds]\nTurn 35. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - writing] Student begins to write the answer for problem 5, starting with the number 3.\nTurn 35. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 29 seconds] The student has written \"98,603\" on the board.\nTutor highlights the word \"NOT\" in red.\nTutor circles the hexagon shape in green.\nTutor circles the number \"1\" next to the first problem.\nTurn 35. TUTOR: Okay, are you done?\nTurn 36. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 37. TUTOR: Okay, let's go through them together.\nTurn 38. TUTOR: So, can you read me the first problem, please?\nTurn 39. STUDENT: Which of these does... doesn't... does not have four angles?\nTurn 40. TUTOR: Yeah. And what does four angles mean?\nTurn 41. STUDENT: Four sides.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Yeah, good job. So this one definitely does not have four sides. So you are correct.\nTurn 43. TUTOR: So this is correct. Okay, let's go to the second one.\nTurn 44. TUTOR: Go ahead and read it for me.\nTurn 45. STUDENT: Each box con... contains...\nTurn 46. TUTOR: Contains.\nTurn 47. STUDENT: contains seven chocolates. How many chocolates will be in six box... boxes?\nTurn 48. TUTOR: Boxes. Yeah. Do you know what contains means?\nTurn 49. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 8 seconds] Tutor circles the number \"2\" next to the second problem.\nTutor points to the \"3\" in the fraction \"1/3\".\nTurn 49. TUTOR: It just means each box has seven chocolates. So that each box has seven chocolates inside. Okay?\nTurn 50. TUTOR: So how do you... how did you get forty-two?\nTurn 51. STUDENT: Um... I times it.\nTurn 52. TUTOR: Yeah. And why would you times it?\nTurn 53. STUDENT: 'Cause... 'cause there's six boxes and each box has seven chocolates.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nFor this next reply, decide privately which of the following teaching policies fits the current learner evidence, then apply it. Use the current mathematical task and recent student work in the context of the full conversation. Agreement with a tutor-supplied answer is not proof of understanding. An independently corrected earlier error does not by itself justify more help.\nSCAFFOLDING: Provide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nRIGOR: Push for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
